  "reviews": {
    "r1": {
      "O": {
        "1": {
          "evidence": "The horizontal axis is inscribed 'Advertising Spend ($ thousands)' and the vertical axis is inscribed 'Sales ($ thousands)'.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "2": {
          "evidence": "The five labeled points are plotted at Store 6 (30, 60), Store 7 (35, 68), Store 8 (42, 85), Store 9 (48, 96), and Store 10 (70, 181). Each successive point from Store 6 through Store 10 is positioned farther right and higher on the page than the preceding one.",
          "status": "supported"
        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "If decision-makers may treat the plotted additional top stores as representative of the company and accept a positive spend-sales association as sufficient approval evidence, then the displayed pattern can support company-wide expansion.",
        "evidence": "The chart's title is 'Additional Top Stores: Ad Spend vs Sales', and the plotted points are labeled only Store 6 through Store 10. The chart does not contain data for the full store population, nor does it include any policy text, threshold, or statement that this subset is representative of the company or that a positive association is sufficient for approval. The necessary conditions (representativeness of the subset and sufficiency of the association as approval evidence) are not supported by the visible chart or the provided policy state.",
        "status": "refuted"
      }
    },
    "r2": {
      "O": {
        "1": {
          "evidence": "The chart title at the top of the page reads 'Additional Top Stores: Ad Spend vs Sales'.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "2": {
          "evidence": "Five blue circular data points are visible in the plot area, and each is labeled with text identifying it as Store 6, Store 7, Store 8, Store 9, or Store 10.",
          "status": "supported"
        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "If the chart’s title and attached store labels define its scope as the additional top stores shown, and a company-wide approval requires evidence covering the full store population, then evidence limited to those plotted stores supports requesting full-store analysis rather than approving company-wide expansion.",
        "evidence": "The chart's title ('Additional Top Stores: Ad Spend vs Sales') and the specific labels (Store 6 through Store 10) define the scope of the displayed evidence as a subset of stores. The form option 'Approve company-wide budget expansion' implies a decision based on company-wide evidence. Since the dashboard only provides data for a subset ('Additional Top Stores') and no full-store evidence is supplied, the condition that company-wide approval requires full-population evidence is met. The option 'Hold and request full-store analysis' is a direct response to the limitation of the current evidence to a subset.",
        "status": "supported"
      }
    },
    "r3": {
      "O": {
        "1": {
          "evidence": "The horizontal axis is inscribed 'Advertising Spend ($ thousands)' and the vertical axis is inscribed 'Sales ($ thousands)'.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "2": {
          "evidence": "The five labeled points are plotted at Store 6 (30, 60), Store 7 (35, 68), Store 8 (42, 85), Store 9 (48, 96), and Store 10 (70, 181). Each successive point from Store 6 through Store 10 is positioned farther right and higher on the page than the preceding one.",
          "status": "supported"
        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "If the horizontal axis encodes advertising spend and the vertical axis encodes sales on the printed comparable scales, then points farther right and higher represent stores with greater plotted spend and greater plotted sales; this is an association only, not proof that spend caused sales or that expansion is profitable.",
        "evidence": "The axes are explicitly labeled 'Advertising Spend ($ thousands)' and 'Sales ($ thousands)'. The plotted points show a positive correlation where higher spend corresponds to higher sales for the five stores shown. The statement correctly identifies this as an association based on the visual data and correctly notes that the chart itself does not prove causality or profitability. The conditions of the reading (axis encoding and the nature of the association) are fully supported by the chart's labels and data points.",
        "status": "supported"
      }
    }
  }
}
